## Implemented behavior

`GenericGuardianWindowsRuntimeCoordinator` wraps the already-protected on-demand runtime through `IGenericGuardianWindowsRuntime`. Construction is inert. The loop plan contains only caller-supplied already-resolved inputs. Start is explicit. Cycles are awaited sequentially and cannot overlap.

Stop cancels scheduling, waits for an active cycle and invokes protected runtime reset with `CancellationToken.None`. Cycle failure stops scheduling and attempts reset automatically. Failed automatic cleanup is aggregated with the primary failure and remains retryable via explicit Stop. A failed/completed schedule cannot be restarted until Stop completes the lifecycle boundary. Dispose performs Stop/reset before disposing the runtime and aborts disposal if cleanup fails.

`AppServices.CreateGenericGuardianWindowsRuntimeCoordinator` is explicit only and delegates to the existing runtime factory, preserving the shared Guardian-connected `ControlledBenchmarks` authority. AppServices still does not create/start this coordinator automatically and still has no generic production policy defaults.

## Boundaries and continuation

This infrastructure does not enable automatic generic Guardian. FF/FF MAX remain `CanaryContextEvidence=false`; no production `IGenericGuardianCanaryEvidenceSource` exists; no approved generic production candidate/action mapping/default budget policy exists; external mutation tools remain outside current process-local authority; no real-game HIL or causal FPS benefit is proven.

Next: add a production activation-readiness gate before scheduled Start. It must require truthful adapter context capability, registered production evidence source and explicit approved policy inputs, while current FF/FF MAX remain NotReady. Do not fabricate missing scene/mode/load/environment evidence or policies. Track6 item3 remains IN PROGRESS.

This documentary commit itself requires a fresh exact-head Windows CI before being marked GREEN.
